\name{latents-package}
\alias{latents-package}
\alias{latents}
\docType{package}
\title{Latent Profile, Class and Transition Models}
\description{
\pkg{latents} is an R package for modelling unobserved heterogeneity in
response patterns, regression relationships and trajectories. It supports
latent profile and class analysis, multilevel models, latent transition
analysis, and mixture regression and growth models.

The package combines model estimation and comparison with tools for
assessing parameter uncertainty, classification quality and solution
stability. It also supports analyses relating latent membership to external
variables and provides tables and plots for interpreting and reporting
results.
}
\details{
Latent profiles describe heterogeneity in continuous indicators through
component-specific distributions. Latent classes describe heterogeneity in
discrete response patterns. In the two-level model, observation-level
profiles or classes are nested within groups, and latent group classes
differ in their conditional probabilities of observation-level membership.
The standard two-level formulation shares measurement parameters across
group classes. Transition models additionally estimate dependence between
latent states at successive occasions.

The number of latent classes and the measurement specification are supplied
by the analyst. Estimation uses multiple starting values because different
starts can converge to different local maxima. Convergence, replication of
the selected likelihood, class sizes, parameter boundaries, and substantive
interpretability should be considered together when evaluating a solution.
Classification summaries describe uncertainty under the fitted model;
they do not establish the substantive validity of the classes.
}
\section{Model fitting}{
Use \code{\link{lpa}} or \code{\link{lca}} for single-level models and
\code{\link{multilpa}} or \code{\link{multilca}} for two-level models.
\code{\link{lta}} fits latent transition models, and
\code{\link{mixture_regression}} fits finite mixtures of regression models.
The \code{family} argument of \code{multilpa} provides additional model
families documented on that function's help page.
\code{\link{fit_staged}} estimates a two-level model with measurement
parameters fixed at their first-stage estimates.
}
\section{Model evaluation and inference}{
\code{\link{enumerate_classes}}, \code{\link{enumerate_lpa}},
\code{\link{enumerate_lca}}, and \code{\link{enumerate_regressions}}
fit candidate model sets. \code{\link{compare_models}} summarizes selected
models, and \code{\link{bootstrap_lrt}} provides parametric bootstrap
likelihood ratio tests for supported comparisons.

\code{\link{get_results}} extracts model-specific tables;
\code{\link{diagnostics}} and \code{\link{sensitivity}} assess fitted
solutions and sensitivity to starting values. \code{\link{parameter_inference}}
provides parameter estimates, standard errors, and intervals for supported
model families. \code{\link{three_step}} and \code{\link{r3step}}
relate external variables to latent membership with correction for
classification error. \code{\link{pool_imputations}} combines compatible
analyses across imputed datasets.
}
\section{Interpretation and assumptions}{
The measurement distribution, conditional independence assumptions,
covariance constraints, and treatment of time depend on the selected model.
Consult the fitting function for these assumptions and for restrictions on
missing data, sampling weights, covariates, and parameter inference.
Posterior class assignments are estimates with uncertainty. Class labels
are arbitrary identifiers and should be interpreted through the estimated
response patterns. Numerical fit and classification separation alone do not
establish causal effects or distinct populations.
}
\section{Getting started}{
The vignettes \code{vignette("lpa", package = "latents")},
\code{vignette("lca", package = "latents")},
\code{vignette("enumeration", package = "latents")}, and
\code{vignette("lta", package = "latents")} introduce the main workflows.
See \code{\link{latents-plots}} for graphical summaries,
\code{\link{latents-predict}} for prediction, and
\code{\link{latents-conditions}} for diagnostic conditions.
}
